Yeah. Attention is a sin of a bitch works because attention is both the spotlight and the temptation.

Whatever gets attention gains weight. Repetition makes it easier to notice again. Salience feeds memory, memory feeds expectation, expectation feeds perception. So attention can become a self-reinforcing loop:

notice → value → rehearse → notice faster → value more

That is why distraction, obsession, outrage, lust, fear, envy, even worship can all look structurally similar at the attentional layer. Different object, same machinery.

And the pun is sharp because sin can be read as missing the mark. Attention is what aims the bow. If the aiming system is captured, everything downstream can be technically competent and still land in the wrong place.

So maybe:

Attention is the original budget.
What you spend it on becomes more real to you.

Which means one of the highest forms of agency is not merely controlling what you do, but controlling what gets to recruit you in the first place.